    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Brushes. {StaticResource} works here because it sits in attribute
         position rather than element content — see the note at the top.

         A brush alias exists only where a Brush-typed property needs one that a Color
         cannot serve directly. There is no DmAccentGoldBrush: it was declared with the
         three below, never referenced by anything, and deleted in the adoption pass.
         Every gold stroke in the app names DmAccentGoldLight, which MAUI converts to a
         brush at the point of use. Do not re-add an alias speculatively — add it when a
         property needs it.
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DmBorderTavernBrush" Color="{StaticResource DmBorderTavern}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DmBorderTavernHoverBrush" Color="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernHover}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DmBorderParchmentBrush" Color="{StaticResource DmBorderParchment}" />

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Geometry

         THE RADIUS TOKENS ARE NOW CONSUMED, AND THE RATCHET KEEPS THEM THAT WAY.
         An earlier revision of this note said they were deliberately unconsumed and
         that migrating them was held for a later sweep "because no hygiene pattern
         covers radii". Both halves are now out of date: GeometryScaleTests.
         NoBareRadiusValues is that pattern, and Controls/ and the implicit styles
         have been converted. Views/ is allow-listed in that fact until the wave that
         owns it removes its entries.

         Every literal that was converted was already exactly 2, 3 or 4, so those
         conversions are pixel-neutral by construction. ONE IS NOT, and it is
         deliberate: the implicit Button style's radius moved from 8 to DmRadiusLoose
         (4), so every unstyled Button in the app changed shape to match the surfaces
         around it. That reasoning is recorded at the style itself in Implicit.xaml.

         Two literals survive on purpose, and each is named in the ratchet rather
         than merely left alone: DungeonListPage's CornerRadius 26, which is half the
         FAB's own 52px height and so is geometry rather than style, and
         DmBorderBase's string-form 3 — see the setter note below.

         BEFORE ANYONE RELIES ON IT: Controls.Base.xaml says StrokeShape
         "unavoidably" needs a literal. That is TRUE OF THE STRING FORM ONLY. The
         element form puts the radius in attribute position, where a StaticResource
         does resolve:

             <Border.StrokeShape>
                 <RoundRectangle CornerRadius="{StaticResource DmRadiusDefault}" />
             </Border.StrokeShape>

         and the element form is already used three times in the tree
         (ControlsPaletteView.xaml:36, ObjectPaletteView.xaml:14,
         SecondaryPaletteView.xaml:12), all with a literal 4.

         SPIKED AND CONFIRMED WORKING, 2026-08-07. The x:Double resolves, converts
         through CornerRadius's implicit double conversion, and REACHES THE SCREEN —
         it does not silently land on 0 or a default. Evidence: DmSearchBar's Border
         was moved to the element form bound to a temporary 20-valued radius token,
         built, LAUNCHED, and screenshotted; its corner arc was unmistakably 20
         against the DmCard directly below it still carrying the literal 4, then
         rebound to DmRadiusLoose and confirmed pixel-identical to the pre-change
         frame. Build success alone was NOT taken as the answer: Task 10 of the theme
         migration had a resource reference build clean AND launch clean and still
         throw on first lookup, because WinUI creates dictionary entries lazily.

         BUT THE STYLE SETTER IS A DIFFERENT CASE, AND IT IS THE EXCEPTION. The spike
         above covers a Border ELEMENT. Putting an element-form shape inside a
         <Setter> was spiked separately and FAILED on behaviour, not on binding: the
         shape is built once when the dictionary is parsed and the SAME INSTANCE is
         handed to every Border adopting the style. Two Borders returned
         ReferenceEquals true, both reported the LAST Border as the shape's Parent,
         and mutating the radius through one visibly changed what the other rendered.
         DmBorderBase therefore KEEPS its string-form literal, the reasoning is
         recorded in full at Controls.Base.xaml, and
         BorderBaseStrokeShapeRadius_EqualsDmRadiusDefault stays — it is the only
         thing keeping that literal in step with DmRadiusDefault. Do not delete it.
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmRadiusTight">2</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="DmRadiusDefault">3</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="DmRadiusLoose">4</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="DmStrokeHairline">1</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="DmStrokeEmphasis">2</x:Double>
    <!-- The outline-CANCEL border weight, and the only value on this ladder that is not an
         integer. It is not drift: it was authored twice, on the same role, before any theme
         work (DmConfirmPopup.xaml:36 and CreateDungeonPopup.xaml:74 — both the CANCEL button
         of a two-button popup row, whose sibling is a FILLED button). On an outline button the
         border carries the whole visual weight, so a heavier stroke than the hairline used for
         incidental separators is a real design decision, and this token records it rather than
         letting a magic 1.5 sit at two call sites. It has exactly those two consumers; if a
         reviewer decides the role does not warrant its own weight, collapse both to
         DmStrokeHairline and DELETE this token rather than leaving it orphaned. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmStrokeMedium">1.5</x:Double>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Geometry: the spacing scale

         Seven steps — 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 20 — for Spacing, RowSpacing and ColumnSpacing.
         Bind them in ATTRIBUTE position, which is proven to resolve; see the spike note
         below the padding roles.

         WHY SEVEN AND NOT SIX: 6 is in genuine use at six sites and forcing it to 4 or 8 is a
         33% jump on a tight icon/label row.

         WHY WORDS AND NOT NUMBERS: DmSpace4 would bake a value into its own name, so a
         rescale would have to touch every call site to stay honest. Nothing else in this
         theme names itself after its value — DmRadiusTight, DmStrokeHairline,
         DmFontSizeBody, DmIconSizeSmall — and the ramp below deliberately reuses
         DmIconSize's own vocabulary (Small / Medium / Large / XL) extended symmetrically at
         both ends, so the two double-valued ramps read the same way.

         ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         SNAPPING TABLE — the applying wave's ONLY source for these decisions.
         Fixed by docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-07-design-system-completion-design.md §3.1.
         Do not re-derive them per call site; a second opinion here is how a scale drifts.

             0  →  0   KEEP AS A LITERAL. "No spacing" is the absence of a scale value,
                       not the smallest one, and giving it a token invites DmSpaceNone to
                       be used where a real step was meant.
             2  →  2   already on scale
             3  →  4   DmSpaceXS
             4  →  4   already on scale
             5  →  4   DmSpaceXS — six sites, all icon/label rows
             6  →  6   already on scale
             7  →  8   DmSpaceMedium
             8  →  8   already on scale
             9  →  8   DmSpaceMedium
            10  →  12  DmSpaceLarge — button rows; 12 reads better against its neighbours
            14  →  12  DmSpaceLarge
            16  →  16  already on scale
            20  →  20  already on scale

         Note the two directions at 10 and 14: 10 rounds UP and 14 rounds DOWN, both onto 12.
         That is intended — it collapses the button-row cluster onto one step — and it is why
         this table exists rather than a "snap to nearest" rule.
         ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- Hairline gaps inside a single control: a glyph and its caption. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceXXS">2</x:Double>
    <!-- Related items in one visual group: label above its field, icon beside its text. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceXS">4</x:Double>
    <!-- A tight row that still needs to read as separate items: stacked tool captions. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceSmall">6</x:Double>
    <!-- The default gap between sibling controls in a row or column. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceMedium">8</x:Double>
    <!-- Between button-row buttons, and between fields in a form. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceLarge">12</x:Double>
    <!-- Between distinct groups within one panel or page section. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceXL">16</x:Double>
    <!-- Between major page sections; the widest deliberate gap on the scale. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="DmSpaceXXL">20</x:Double>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Geometry: the padding roles

         ROLES, NOT A SCALE. 30 distinct Padding values exist today, many legitimately
         asymmetric (an inset that shapes one icon, a notebook pane's four-value tuck). A flat
         numeric scale would either flatten that real intent or fail to cover it, so these are
         named after the SURFACE they pad. Pick the role, not the number.

         Everything not covered snaps to the nearest role. Asymmetric four-value paddings
         survive as documented exceptions and each must carry a one-line comment saying what
         it is shaping; an undocumented asymmetric padding is a defect.

         SPIKE RESULT (2026-08-07, verified at RUNTIME, not merely at build):
         a Thickness token DOES resolve from {StaticResource} in attribute position. Proved by
         binding DmSearchBar's Border to a temporary 40,30 Thickness and to a temporary
         RoundRectangle CornerRadius of 20 in the ELEMENT form, launching the app, and
         observing both the fat inset and the large corner arc against the DmCard below it,
         which still carried the literal 4. A silent default would have rendered 0 padding and
         a square corner; neither happened. A clean build alone would NOT have shown this —
         Platforms/Windows/App.xaml once built clean, launched clean, and then threw
         COMException on the first actual lookup, so build success is not evidence here.
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- Compact icon buttons: the glyph is the content and the box only needs to be hittable. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingTight">4,2</Thickness>
    <!-- Standard action buttons and tool chips. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingCompact">8,4</Thickness>
    <!-- Text inputs — Entry, Editor and SearchBar surfaces. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingControl">10,4</Thickness>
    <!-- Card and list-row surfaces. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingCard">12,10</Thickness>
    <!-- Popup bodies and palette panels. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingPanel">14,10</Thickness>
    <!-- Page roots — the outermost inset of a ContentPage. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DmPaddingPage">16,12</Thickness>

    <!-- There is deliberately no toolbar-height token here any more.
         DmToolbarHeight (54.4) existed only because the designer's map toolbar and its sidebar
         dungeon card were row 0 of two SEPARATE sub-grids, so nothing but a shared number could
         make them agree — and that number had to be read off a screenshot, which meant it went
         stale silently whenever the toolbar's contents grew. Both now sit in ONE Auto row of the
         parent grid in DungeonDetailPage.xaml, which sizes itself to the taller of the two at the
         first frame with no measurement at all. -->

</ResourceDictionary>
